---
title: Borscht
description: Beetroot soup with potato, carrot and white beans, finished with vinegar and dill. Made with vegetable stock.
author: Natasha Kravchuk
source: https://natashaskitchen.com/classic-russian-borscht-recipe/
course: soup
cuisine: eastern european
diet: vegan
difficulty: medium
servings: 10
time: 1h10m
prep time: 30m
cook time: 40m
tags: [beetroot, beans, one-pot, make-ahead, freezer-friendly]
---

Prep all the vegetables before you start. Keep the cut potatoes in a #bowl of cold water so they don't discolour, and drain just before using.

> Wear gloves when peeling and grating the beetroot, or your fingertips stay red for days. A food processor grates more coarsely than a box grater, so the beetroot keeps some texture.

Heat @olive oil{2%tbsp} in a #large soup pot{} (5 l or larger) over medium-high heat. Add @beetroot{3}(medium, peeled and grated) and sauté ~{10%minutes}, stirring occasionally, until softened.

Add @vegetable stock{2%l} and @water{500%ml}, then the @potatoes{3}(medium Yukon Gold or other waxy, peeled and cut into bite-sized pieces) and @carrots{2}(peeled and thinly sliced). Cook ~potatoes{10%minutes} (up to 15), until easily pierced with a fork.

If using cabbage, add @?white cabbage{0.25}(small head, thinly shredded; up to half a head) when the potatoes are halfway cooked.

Meanwhile, heat @olive oil{2%tbsp} in a #large frying pan{} over medium-high heat. Add @onion{1}(medium, finely chopped), @celery stalks{2}(finely chopped) and @?red bell pepper{1}(small, finely chopped). Sauté ~zazharka{7%minutes} (up to 8), stirring occasionally, until softened and lightly golden. \
Stir in @ketchup{4%tbsp}(or 3 tbsp tomato passata), fry ~{30%seconds}, then tip it all into the soup pot.

When the potatoes and carrots are soft, add @cannellini beans{1%tin}(400 g, with their liquid), @bay leaves{2}, @white vinegar{2%tbsp}, @sea salt{1%tsp}, @black pepper{0.25%tsp}(freshly ground), @garlic clove{1}(large, pressed) and @dill{3%tbsp}(chopped). Simmer ~{2%minutes} (up to 3), then adjust with more salt and up to another tablespoon of vinegar to taste.

Serve with a spoonful of @?sour cream{to serve}.

> Keeps 4 to 5 days in the fridge and tastes better the next day. Freezes for up to 3 months, though the potatoes soften a little on thawing.
